    "body": "The Disko project in Greenland. Image: 80 Mile.\nBy: Staff Writer November 17, 2026\nGreenland Energy (Nasdaq: GLND) agreed to acquire 80 Mile (LSE-A: 80M) on Nov. 12 in an all-share deal valued at about £74 million (US$98 million), bringing the partners’ Greenland oil exploration interests and 80 Mile’s mineral assets under one Nasdaq-listed group.\n80 Mile shareholders would receive 0.0079485 Greenland Energy shares for each share held, implying a value of 1.28 pence. That represents a 28% premium to 80 Mile’s Nov. 11 closing price and a 71% premium to its Sept. 7 close, before the offer period began.\n“This combination builds on our existing partnership and brings our respective Jameson Land interests under common ownership,” Greenland Energy CEO Robert Price said in the announcement. “It would simplify the development structure.”\nThe acquisition would broaden Greenland Energy’s portfolio beyond oil exploration into minerals and Italian biodiesel production. Its plans include a strategic review of 80 Mile’s Dundas ilmenite project within six months of completion, potentially leading to a farm-out or sale.\nGreenland Energy shares were 2.5% stronger at US$2.04 Tuesday morning in New York, valuing the company at about US$89 million. In London, 80 Mile was 2.5% weaker at 0.858 pence.\nFunding, permits\nThe fully diluted valuation was calculated using Greenland Energy’s Nov. 11 closing price of US$2.14 and an exchange rate of US$1.33 per pound. The value of the share consideration will fluctuate with Greenland Energy’s stock and exchange rates. Each company’s shareholders would own about half of the combined group.\nGreenland Energy had US$22.7 million in cash and equivalents at Sept. 30, plus US$4.3 million in marketable equity securities, it disclosed in a Nov. 16 filing . Its cash balance was down from US$37.4 million at the end of June. The company raised about US$70 million before fees in April.\nGreenland Energy has also agreed to provide 80 Mile with a £3-million unsecured convertible loan carrying 12% annual interest and a 12-month term. The funding is intended to bridge a cash shortfall: 80 Mile’s existing resources are insufficient to execute its strategy through completion, particularly the ramp-up of its Italian biodiesel operations, the companies said.\nDisko-Nuussuaq\n80 Mile’s minerals portfolio also includes Disko-Nuussuaq in West Greenland, which targets nickel, copper, cobalt and platinum group elements. Drilling began July 1 under a joint venture with USFM Corp., which agreed to fund US$30 million to earn up to 51%. Dundas is in northwest Greenland, while the group’s Italian assets include the Greenswitch biodiesel plant.\nThe partners’ Jameson Land Basin project is an onshore oil exploration venture in East Greenland. Greenland Energy has the right to earn up to 70% by funding the first two exploration wells.\nThe planned 2026 drilling program has been deferred, and the partners are targeting a permitting timeline in winter 2027. Greenland Energy said it is reviewing contractor commitments, equipment procurement and logistics to preserve cash during the delay.\nThe acquisition is expected to close in the first half of 2027, subject to shareholder and court approvals, Nasdaq approval for the new shares and applicable regulatory consents, including from Greenland’s government.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: cobalt Nickel Oil and Gas Platinum Group Metals\nREGION: Europe\nCOMPANY: 80 Mile Greenland Energy\nRepublish this article",
